  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nLow pressure, now centered over NE Ohio/NW PA, is expected to \ncontinue to trek across the Northeast throughout today and \nSaturday. This will bring showery conditions across eastern New \nYork and western New England throughout that time. With above \naverage temperatures making a return today through early next \nweek, much of this precipitation will remain as rain. Some wet \nsnow could mix in over higher terrain areas, with marginal \ntemperatures at the surface and aloft, but little to no snow \naccumulation is expected. Rain accumulations are also expected \nto remain below a tenth of an inch across the region through \nSaturday. Weak ridging builds in overnight Saturday, providing \nsome partial clearing and a break from the rain, before the next\nsystem and chance of precipitation arrives overnight Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next system approaching from the west, is expected to bring\nanother round of mainly rain Sun night into Mon, along with \ngusty winds. Latest guidance is indicating a somewhat stronger \nsystem, but still a progressive open wave trough aloft, with a \nsurface cyclone passing just north/west of our area. Due to \nquick movement and more limited moisture, rainfall amounts will \nbe less than 1 inch. Latest NBM probabilities for > 0.50\" rain \nare 20-80%, with the highest chances in favored upslope areas of\nthe SW. Adirondacks and S. Greens. Probs. for > 1.00\" rain are \nonly 20-40% in the upslope areas though. So no flooding is \nanticipated. Temperatures should be mild enough for plain rain \nfor much of the time, although rain will change to snow showers \nin the W. Adirondacks Mon P.M. as colder air filters in with \nlake enhanced/upslope snow showers lingering. There could be a \nminor dusting of accumulation, but probs. for > 1\" are less than\n20%. Behind the system's cold front, northwest winds will \nstrengthen substantially as the surface cyclone deepens \nconsiderably in E. New England which will increase the pressure \ngradient. Cold advection will promote decent mixing. Probs for \ngusts > 40 mph have increased to 30-70% in the Capital District,\nRensselaer Plateau and Berkshires. If confidence in > 45 mph \ngusts increases, a Wind Advisory would be needed.",
